<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The launch field in dark mode.

      Android picks this over values/splash.xml whenever the system is in dark
      mode, so the launch follows the device instead of flashing a white screen at
      someone who has asked every app not to.

      Black, because that is what the default background became when the ground
      turned into a preference (Settings → Appearance). This file is compiled into
      the APK and cannot read a preference, so only the default can hand over to
      the app invisibly — someone who picks Navy or Charcoal sees this field for
      the moment before the WebView paints. Tracking the default is the best a
      launch theme can do, and it is why `mono` is the default rather than the navy
      the app used to ship.

      Keep in step with the splash-bg custom property in globals.css and the
      `mono` dark ramp in lib/surfaceTheme. (NativeShell only sets the status bar
      style, the icon tint, so there is no colour there to match.)

      The orange launcher mark drawn on top reads clearly on both fields, which is
      why one icon serves both themes.
    -->
    <color name="splashBackground">#000000</color>
</resources>
